This version uses the same binary search idea but removes the duplicate problem one boundary at a time. If the left value and middle value are equal, the left value gives no useful information, so `left` is increased and the loop continues.

The important check for `nums[mid] == k` comes first. That prevents skipping a matching value. An input such as `[7]` is also handled without a separate case.

```python
class Solution:
    def searchInARotatedSortedArrayII(self, nums, k):
        left = 0
        right = len(nums) - 1

        while left <= right:
            mid = left + (right - left) // 2

            if nums[mid] == k:
                return True

            if nums[left] == nums[mid]:
                left += 1
                continue

            if nums[left] <= nums[mid]:
                if nums[left] <= k < nums[mid]:
                    right = mid - 1
                else:
                    left = mid + 1
            else:
                if nums[mid] < k <= nums[right]:
                    left = mid + 1
                else:
                    right = mid - 1

        return False
```

The average time is `O(log n)`. If almost every value is the same, the repeated boundary skips make the worst case `O(n)`. Space usage is `O(1)`, and the input list is not modified.

A modified binary search still works, but equal values at both ends need special handling. When `nums[left]`, `nums[mid]`, and `nums[right]` are equal, there is no way to tell which side is sorted, so both boundaries move inward.

Dry run: for `[2, 5, 6, 0, 0, 1, 2]` with `k = 0`, the left side is sorted at first, so the search moves right. Later `mid` lands on `0`, and the method returns `true`.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean searchInARotatedSortedArrayII(int[] nums, int k) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = nums.length - 1;

        while (left <= right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

            if (nums[mid] == k) {
                return true;
            }

            if (nums[left] == nums[mid] && nums[mid] == nums[right]) {
                left++;
                right--;
                continue;
            }

            if (nums[left] <= nums[mid]) {
                if (nums[left] <= k && k < nums[mid]) {
                    right = mid - 1;
                } else {
                    left = mid + 1;
                }
            } else {
                if (nums[mid] < k && k <= nums[right]) {
                    left = mid + 1;
                } else {
                    right = mid - 1;
                }
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```

The usual running time is `O(log n)`, but many duplicates can reduce it to `O(n)`. The extra space is `O(1)`. The array stays unchanged.

The rotation does not matter if all values are placed in a set first. Then checking `k` is just a membership lookup. This also gives a simple option when keeping the binary search conditions readable is more important than saving memory.

Dry run: for `[2, 5, 6, 0, 0, 1, 2]`, the set contains `0`, so searching for `0` returns `true`. Searching for `4` returns `false` after the set is built.

```go
func searchInARotatedSortedArrayII(nums []int, k int) bool {
    values := make(map[int]struct{}, len(nums))

    for _, value := range nums {
        values[value] = struct{}{}
    }

    _, found := values[k]
    return found
}
```

Building the map takes `O(n)` average time, and the lookup is `O(1)` average time. The total extra space is `O(n)` because the distinct values are stored. The original slice is not modified.

Kept this one direct with a linear scan. Rotation and duplicate values do not change the basic check: return `true` as soon as one value equals `k`.

For example, with `[2, 5, 6, 0, 0, 1, 2]` and `k = 0`, the loop reaches the first `0` and stops. If `k = 3`, every value is checked and the result is `false`.

