class Solution {
public:
    bool searchInARotatedSortedArrayII(vector<int> &nums, int k)  {
        for (int value : nums) {
            if (value == k) {
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The input array is not changed. This is not as fast as binary search on clear inputs, but it is very easy to verify and handles an empty array too.

Another way is to find the rotation point first, then use normal binary search on the two sorted pieces. The scan finds the first index where the current value is smaller than the previous value. If no such index exists, the array is already sorted.

This handles duplicates without trying to guess the sorted side during every search step. The trade-off is that finding the pivot takes a full pass.

```cpp
class Solution {
private:
    bool binarySearch(vector<int> &nums, int left, int right, int k) {
        while (left <= right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

            if (nums[mid] == k) {
                return true;
            }
            if (nums[mid] < k) {
                left = mid + 1;
            } else {
                right = mid - 1;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }

public:
    bool searchInARotatedSortedArrayII(vector<int> &nums, int k)  {
        int n = nums.size();
        if (n == 0) {
            return false;
        }

        int pivot = n;
        for (int i = 1; i < n; ++i) {
            if (nums[i] < nums[i - 1]) {
                pivot = i;
                break;
            }
        }

        if (binarySearch(nums, 0, pivot - 1, k)) {
            return true;
        }
        return binarySearch(nums, pivot, n - 1, k);
    }
};
```

For `[4, 5, 6, 1, 2, 3]`, the pivot is index `3`, so the searches use `[4, 5, 6]` and `[1, 2, 3]`. A single value and an already sorted array are also valid edge cases.

The pivot scan costs `O(n)`, followed by `O(log n)` searches. Overall time is `O(n)` and extra space is `O(1)`. The array is only read, not changed.

